The netplay thread no longer writes to the SD card in a match, and it times its own loop (patches/net-0016).
- net_diag kept network.csv open from the first online frame and the netplay thread (the one that sends our inputs and
  handles the opponent's) wrote and flushed a row every second: on the console, a write to the SD card from that thread
  in every match. The rows are now kept in memory and written when the game is reported (or the connection is cleaned
  up), on a thread of their own; the game thread no longer opens the file at the first online frame either. The file gets
  three more columns (the worst queue-to-socket time of our inputs, the longest held-up step of the netplay thread and its
  name); a file with the old header is kept as network-old.csv.
- Each step of the netplay thread's loop is timed. An input that reaches the socket 20 ms or more after the game queued it
  is logged with the longest step meanwhile ("slippi: netplay thread: our input left ..."), and 100 ms or more without a
  packet from the opponent is logged with whether this side's thread was free at the time (then the delay was on the
  network or the opponent's side) or held up, and by what. A summary line at the game's end: queue to socket p50, p99,
  worst, late sends, silences.
- MELEE_NET_TEST_THREAD_STALL=FRAME:MS (tests only) holds the netplay thread MS ms once at online frame FRAME.
